## demography

The U.S. Census Bureau reported 331,449,281 residents on April 1, 2020,[^10][^11] making the United States the third-most-populous country in the world, after India and China.[^12] The Census Bureau's official 2025 population estimate was 341,784,857, an increase of 3.1% since the 2020 census.[^13] According to the Bureau's U.S. Population Clock, on July 1, 2024, the U.S. population had a net gain of one person every 16 seconds, or about 5400 people per day.[^14] In 2023, 51% of Americans age 15 and over were married, 6% were widowed, 10% were divorced, and 34% had never been married.[^15] In 2023, the total fertility rate for the U.S. stood at 1.6 children per woman,[^16] and, at 23%, it had the world's highest rate of children living in single-parent households in 2019.[^17] Most Americans live in the suburbs of major metropolitan areas. ([Demographics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States#Demographics))

