## culture

The United States is home to a wide variety of ethnic groups, traditions, and customs.[^25][^26] The country has been described as having the values of individualism and personal autonomy,[^27][^28] as well as a strong work ethic[^29] and competitiveness.[^30] Voluntary altruism toward others also plays a major role;[^31][^32][^33] according to a 2016 study by the Charities Aid Foundation, Americans donated 1.44% of total GDP to charity—the highest rate in the world by a large margin.[^34] Americans have traditionally been characterized by a unifying political belief in an "American Creed" that emphasizes consent of the governed, liberty, equality under the law, democracy, social equality, property rights, and a preference for limited government.[^35][^36][^37] The U.S. has acquired significant hard and soft power through its diplomatic influence, economic power, military alliances, and cultural exports such as American movies, music, video games, sports, and food.[^38][^39] The influence that the United States exerts on other countries through soft power is referred to as Americanization.[^40] ([Culture and society](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States#Culture_and_society))

